#!/usr/bin/env bash
# promote-released-cards — move a release's tracking cards to a "released" stage.
#
# Generic across repos (sibling to release-pr-body). Board / stage come from
# `.release-pr.json` (`.promote` block); the API BASE comes from $KANBAN_API_BASE, falling
# back to `.promote.api_base`; the API token comes from $KANBAN_WRITEBACK_TOKEN and is only
# ever sent to a base that passed the $KANBAN_EXPECTED_HOST guard (see ENVIRONMENT below).
# A card is "shipped in this release" if its
# `payload.dl_number` matches a shipped DL token, its `payload.pr_number` matches a
# shipped PR number, or its own `id` is in an explicitly-passed `--cards` set — so
# cards tracked only by PR (no DL) are promoted too.
# Idempotent (skips a card already at target), best-effort per card.
# With the opt-in `--shipped-stages IDS` guard, a matched card NOT currently in a
# Shipped-class source stage is skipped, not moved (promotion never resurrects a
# declined/backlog card carrying a stale/recycled stamp); see the Usage note below.
#
# Shipped refs come from an EXPLICIT set (`--dls "DL-1,DL-2"` and/or `--cards "12,34"`),
# else from `git log <base>..<head>`:
#   - DL tokens via `ref_token_regex` (default DL-[0-9]+), matched on the numeric part
#     against a card's `payload.dl_number`;
#   - PR numbers from each commit's trailing `(#NNN)` (the squash-merge marker GitHub appends),
#     matched against `payload.pr_number`;
#   - card IDs from `--cards` only, matched against the card's own `id`.
#
# WHY CARD IDS ARE EXPLICIT-ONLY AND NEVER DERIVED FROM COMMIT SUBJECTS. A `card#NNNN`
# token in a subject is not reliably a claim that the commit SHIPS that card — descriptive
# citations ("supersedes card#1234", "sibling of card#5678") are common and are already
# known to hijack correlation elsewhere. For a DL token that misfire is bounded by the
# card also needing a matching `dl_number` stamp; for an id it is not — the id IS the
# match, so a passing mention would relocate an unrelated card. Deriving ids from subjects
# is therefore left to the caller, and the one caller that does it (release-pr-body's
# read-only coverage section) passes them with --dry-run, where the worst outcome is a
# coverage line that reads "covered". Do not "complete" this by adding a
# `card_token_regex` read to the derive path below.
#
# Robustness (these states are silent-but-wrong, so they're made loud):
#   - 0 visible cards on page 1 => FAIL (token's user not a board member / wrong instance).
#   - Reads the WHOLE board by paging (`page=N`), so a board >200 cards is not truncated.
#   - A 2xx page carrying no card ARRAY => FAIL, on ANY page (card#6630): `.data // []` turned
#     an unreadable later page into `[]`, which is a short page, which ENDED the scan — so the
#     tool promoted from a board it had only partly read, at rc 0.
#   - A server SHORT READ (pages deliver fewer rows than the board's own meta.total) => FAIL:
#     promoting from an incomplete scan would silently leave a shipped card un-promoted.
#   - A shipped ref that matched NO card is reported at the end (a card may be unstamped).
#   - Nothing NEWLY promoted despite a non-empty shipped-ref set => the exit is decided
#     by the ROOT CAUSE: a MERGE-commit release tip (or an explicit --dls/--cards set) means
#     ref-derivation was COMPLETE, so the refs are genuinely cardless => WARN (exit 0); a
#     non-merge tip means
#     a squash/rebase merge may have dropped the per-PR refs (the v0.32.0 silent-green class)
#     => FAIL (exit 2). This converges two divergent prior behaviors: the bridge copy
#     blanket-failed any 0-promoted run (DL-165), the toolkit/kanban copies exited 0
#     silently — neither distinguished the cause.
#   - Assumes it fires on a RELEASE merge to a protected main (push==release-merge). A plain
#     non-merge commit pushed directly to main whose range carries cardless DLs would fail
#     loud by the same rule — intended, given the assumption.
#
# Usage: promote-released-cards [--dls LIST] [--cards IDS] [--base REF] [--head REF] [--shipped-stages IDS] [--dry-run] [--config PATH]
#
# --cards "12,34": comma-separated numeric CARD IDS, correlated against each card's own
#   `id` (not against dl_number/pr_number). Opt-in and additive — ABSENT is the only way
#   to select the prior behavior, and with it absent every run is byte-identical to
#   before. Combinable with --dls; either flag selects the explicit-ref path, so no git
#   range is read. Strictly numeric: `--cards "card#12"` is a config error, not a parse.
#
# --shipped-stages "51,52": comma-separated numeric workflow_stage_ids that count as a
#   "Shipped-class" source stage. When set, a DL/PR-matched card whose CURRENT stage is
#   NOT in this set is SKIPPED (with a log line) rather than moved — promotion means
#   Shipped→Released and must never resurrect a declined/backlog card carrying a stale or
#   recycled DL/PR stamp. Absent (default) = no source-stage guard, byte-identical prior
#   behavior — consumers opt in. ABSENT is the only way to select that default: an
#   explicitly EMPTY value (`--shipped-stages ""`) is a config error and dies, like any
#   other malformed set — it never silently degrades to the unguarded default.
#
# ENVIRONMENT (all three are read at run time; none has a baked default):
#
# --- KANBAN_WRITEBACK_TOKEN (REQUIRED) --------------------------------------------------
#   The bearer token the card PATCHes are made with. Absent => refuse before any request.
#
# --- KANBAN_EXPECTED_HOST (REQUIRED) ----------------------------------------------------
#   The one host that token may be sent to. The resolved api_base must be https:// on it, or
#   on a subdomain of it; anything else refuses before the first request. Pin it in the
#   promote CI env as a repo/org VARIABLE, not in the repo.
#
# --- KANBAN_API_BASE (optional) ---------------------------------------------------------
#   The api base to promote against, e.g. https://kanban.example.com/api/v3. It OVERRIDES
#   `.promote.api_base`; unset or empty means "use the committed value".
#
#   WHY IT EXISTS. The committed `.promote.api_base` is normally a host-scrubbed placeholder
#   — the real kanban host does not belong in a repo, least of all a vendored one — so the
#   config alone could name the guard's CONSTRAINT ($KANBAN_EXPECTED_HOST, out-of-band) but
#   not its TARGET, and the two could never agree: every run refused, for everyone, and the
#   only ways to run the tool were to commit the real host or carry a permanent uncommitted
#   edit to a security-sensitive file (card#7494).
#
#   WHY IT IS NOT A HOLE IN THE GUARD. The value is host-validated by exactly the same
#   host_ok call as the config value — one resolution, one guard, no second path — so an
#   env-supplied base pointing off $KANBAN_EXPECTED_HOST refuses identically. An env var is
#   not trusted here for being an env var; it is trusted for the same reason the config
#   value is, which is that it survived the guard. What being out-of-band buys is the
#   ATTACKER side: a PR can edit `.release-pr.json`, so it could always move the target and
#   never the constraint; with both supplied from the environment it can move NEITHER.
set -euo pipefail

die() { echo "promote-released-cards: $*" >&2; exit 2; }
for c in jq curl git; do command -v "$c" >/dev/null || die "$c is required"; done

# require_value <flag> <value>: die unless a value-taking flag was given a non-empty value.
# An option that consumes "$2" must dispatch on the FLAG being SEEN, not on its VALUE being
# non-empty — otherwise `--flag ""` (an unexpanded shell variable, an empty CI input) is
# indistinguishable from the flag being absent and silently selects the DEFAULT path. For
# --shipped-stages that default is "no source-stage guard", so an empty value produced an
# UNGUARDED promote with a summary line byte-identical to a guarded one (card#5144). None of
# these flags has a meaningful empty value; an intentional "none" would be a spelled
# sentinel. Also catches a trailing flag with no argument at all, which `set -u` would
# otherwise report as an unbound-variable error naming no flag.
# Mirrors kb_require_value in _kb-board-lib.sh (card#5146) — this script is vendored
# standalone into consumer repos and MUST NOT source that lib, so the guard is duplicated;
# keep the two in sync, as with host_ok and numlist. release-pr-body carries the third copy
# for the same reason.
require_value() { [ -n "${2:-}" ] || die "$1 requires a non-empty value"; }

# in_shipped_stage <stage-id>: true iff the id is in the comma-separated $SHIPPED_STAGES set.
# Only consulted when SHIPPED_STAGES is non-empty (the guard is opt-in).
in_shipped_stage() { case ",$SHIPPED_STAGES," in *",$1,"*) return 0 ;; *) return 1 ;; esac; }

# uint_ok <s> / uint_csv_ok <s>: true iff <s> is a non-empty run of ASCII digits, or a
# non-empty comma-separated list of them (no leading/trailing/doubled comma).
#
# WHY THE LC_ALL — the non-obvious constraint. A bracket RANGE in a bash pattern is a
# COLLATION range, not an ASCII range: it means "every character that sorts between these
# two in the CURRENT locale". Under an ordinary en_US.UTF-8 shell, `case $x in *[!0-9]*)`
# does NOT fire for U+0663 ARABIC-INDIC DIGIT THREE — measured — so a board_id /
# released_stage_id / --shipped-stages / meta.total carrying one passed a check whose
# message says "numeric". Writing the class out longhand also works; LC_ALL=C is used
# because it is one mechanism for both the digit and CSV shapes. `local` scopes it to the
# call and restores it on return. Mirrors kb_ere_match/kb_is_uint in _kb-board-lib.sh —
# this script is vendored standalone into consumer repos and MUST NOT source that lib, so
# the guard is duplicated; keep the two in sync, as with require_value and host_ok.
uint_ok()     { local LC_ALL=C; case "${1:-}" in ''|*[!0-9]*) return 1 ;; esac; }
uint_csv_ok() { local LC_ALL=C; case "${1:-}" in ''|*[!0-9,]*|,*|*,|*,,*) return 1 ;; esac; }

# host_ok <api_base>: true iff api_base is https:// and its host is $EXPECT_HOST or a
# subdomain of it. Assumes $EXPECT_HOST is non-empty (the caller enforces that before
# calling — see the required-var check below). Host is parsed per RFC 3986 — the authority
# ends at the FIRST of '/', '?' or '#', then userinfo before the last '@' is stripped, then
# :port (but see the IP-literal case below, which ends at ']' instead) — so
# `https://good@evil/` (→ evil), `https://good.evil/`, and
# `https://evil#@good.host` (which terminating at '/' alone let through, because the
# userinfo strip then reached into the fragment while curl discarded it) are all rejected.
# The parser MUST agree with curl about where the authority ends. Mirrors
# kb_require_https_host in _kb-board-lib.sh — this script is vendored standalone into
# consumer repos and MUST NOT source that lib, so the guard is duplicated; keep the two in
# sync, INCLUDING the required-var check. (#3570)
host_ok() {
  case "$1" in https://*) ;; *) return 1 ;; esac
  local h="${1#https://}"; h="${h%%[/?#]*}"; h="${h##*@}"
  # The port strip CANNOT start at the first ':' — a bracketed IPv6 literal is nothing but
  # colons, so `[::1]:8080` cut there yields `[`, and the refusal then names `[` as the host
  # to declare, which matches EVERY bracketed authority. RFC 3986 ends the IP-literal at ']'
  # and curl agrees, so the host runs through the FIRST ']'. An unclosed '[' keeps the plain
  # strip: curl rejects such a URL outright, so nothing is ever sent with it.
  case "$h" in
    \[*\]*) h="${h%%\]*}]" ;;
    *)      h="${h%%:*}" ;;
  esac
  # ONE trailing dot on either side, not a run: `h.` is the absolute spelling of the same
  # name and resolves identically, while `h..` is not a name at all and must keep failing.
  h="${h%.}"
  # Idempotent: the caller already strips the declared side, so this re-strip is a no-op
  # there. It is here because tests/kb-host-guard-selftest.sh lifts this function OUT of
  # the script and exercises it alone — the extracted unit must carry the whole policy.
  local e="${EXPECT_HOST%.}"
  [ -n "$h" ] || return 1
  # NOT dead code, and NOT a guard the caller already makes. The caller dies on an empty
  # EXPECT_HOST, but it strips the dot BEFORE that test, so the empty case cannot reach here
  # through the script — while the extracted unit has no caller at all. Without this, an
  # EXPECT_HOST of "." leaves e empty and the subdomain arm below becomes `*"."`, which
  # MATCHES any host that still ends in a dot (`h..` strips to `h.`): the unit ACCEPTS where
  # kb_require_https_host REFUSES, and "the two copies agree on every row" is the one thing
  # the selftest exists to assert. Fail closed on a declaration that normalised to nothing.
  [ -n "$e" ] || return 1
  [ "$h" = "$e" ] && return 0
  case "$h" in *".$e") return 0 ;; *) return 1 ;; esac
}

# redact_userinfo <url>: the url with any RFC 3986 USERINFO component replaced by `***`,
# everything else byte-identical; a url with no userinfo comes back UNCHANGED. Mirrors
# kb_redact_url_userinfo in _kb-board-lib.sh — this script is vendored standalone into
# consumer repos and MUST NOT source that lib, so the primitive is duplicated; keep the two
# in sync, as with host_ok and numlist. tests/kb-host-guard-selftest.sh lifts BOTH copies out
# and asserts they agree on every row, exactly as it does for host_ok. (card#7500)
#
# WHY IT EXISTS. host_ok ACCEPTS a base carrying userinfo — `https://u:pw@<expected-host>/`
# is a legitimate api_base and is a pinned accept row — so every message that echoed $API
# printed a password to stderr, i.e. into the CI log of the run that failed. The mask keeps
# the HOST, which is the only thing those messages exist to tell an operator.
#
# ⛔ THE AUTHORITY BOUNDARY IS host_ok's, and it must be. `https://evil.example#@good.host`
# has NO userinfo — the '@' is in the fragment, curl drops the fragment, the authority is
# evil.example, and host_ok refuses it (the #4346 row). Cutting at the last '@' anywhere in
# the string would rewrite that refusal to name good.host as the base being contacted.
redact_userinfo() {
  local u="$1" scheme="" auth rest
  # Scheme stripped only when the string actually STARTS with one: a bare `${u#*://}` matches
  # the FIRST `://` anywhere, so a schemeless `host/p?x://y` would lose everything up to it.
  # ⛔ LC_ALL IS PINNED ON THE LINE BELOW, NOT THREE LINES UP WITH THE OTHER `local`s. A bare
  # bracket RANGE widens under UTF-8 collation, so the pin is required — and
  # tests/locale-range-guard-selftest.sh's static backstop looks for `LC_ALL=C` on the `=~`
  # line or the two above it, which is a WRITING RULE, not a hint: with the pin declared up
  # with the other locals the scanner flagged this line (watched red). The lib's copy gets the
  # same pin from kb_ere_match, which this standalone must not call.
  local LC_ALL=C
  if [[ "$u" =~ ^[A-Za-z][A-Za-z0-9+.-]*:// ]]; then
    scheme="${u%%://*}://"; u="${u#*://}"
  fi
  auth="${u%%[/?#]*}"     # authority ends at the FIRST of / ? # (RFC 3986)
  rest="${u:${#auth}}"
  case "$auth" in
    *@*) auth="***@${auth##*@}" ;;
  esac
  printf '%s' "$scheme$auth$rest"
}

# True iff <ref> is a MERGE commit (>=2 parents). `git rev-list --parents -n1`
# prints "<sha> <parent1> <parent2>..." → >=3 tokens ⇒ merge. A merge-commit release
# tip means the range carries every feature/prep commit subject, so the ref-derivation
# was COMPLETE; a non-merge tip (squash/rebase) may have collapsed it. git failure
# (unknown ref / shallow graft) ⇒ non-merge (fail-loud-safe). CI checks out
# fetch-depth:0, so parents are real.
merge_commit_tip() {
  local line
  line="$(git rev-list --parents -n1 "$1" 2>/dev/null)" || return 1
  [ "$(printf '%s' "$line" | wc -w)" -ge 3 ]
}

CONFIG=".release-pr.json"; DLS_IN=""; CARDS_IN=""; BASE=""; HEAD_REF=""; DRY=0; SHIPPED_STAGES=""
while [ $# -gt 0 ]; do
  case "$1" in
    --dls)            require_value "$1" "${2:-}"; DLS_IN="$2"; shift 2;;
    --cards)          require_value "$1" "${2:-}"; CARDS_IN="$2"; shift 2;;
    --base)           require_value "$1" "${2:-}"; BASE="$2"; shift 2;;
    --head)           require_value "$1" "${2:-}"; HEAD_REF="$2"; shift 2;;
    --shipped-stages) require_value "$1" "${2:-}"; SHIPPED_STAGES="$2"; shift 2;;
    --dry-run)        DRY=1; shift;;
    --config)         require_value "$1" "${2:-}"; CONFIG="$2"; shift 2;;
    # Print the contiguous leading comment block, whatever its length. A FIXED line range
    # silently truncates the moment the header outgrows it — this header reached 43 lines
    # while the range still said 20, so --help stopped before `Usage:`, the one block a
    # --help user needs. Widening the range is not the fix either: it would leak
    # `set -euo pipefail` and the helpers into user-facing output.
    -h|--help) awk 'NR>1 { if (substr($0,1,1) == "#") print; else exit }' "$0"; exit 0;;
    *) die "unknown arg '$1'";;
  esac
done

# Normalize + validate the opt-in source-stage guard set: strip whitespace, then require a
# non-empty comma-separated list of positive integers (mirrors the board_id/stage numeric
# checks). A malformed set is a config error, not a silent no-guard — fail loud.
# require_value has already rejected an explicitly-empty value, so a non-empty
# $SHIPPED_STAGES here means exactly "the flag was passed". The '' arm below is NOT dead
# code: a whitespace-only value (`--shipped-stages " "`) survives require_value and
# normalizes to empty right here, which is the case that arm exists to reject.
if [ -n "$SHIPPED_STAGES" ]; then
  SHIPPED_STAGES="$(printf '%s' "$SHIPPED_STAGES" | tr -d '[:space:]')"
  uint_csv_ok "$SHIPPED_STAGES" \
    || die "--shipped-stages must be a comma-separated list of numeric stage ids (got '$SHIPPED_STAGES')"
fi

# Same normalize-then-validate rule for --cards, and STRICT on purpose. --dls is a token
# stream that gets grepped, so junk in it degrades to "matched nothing"; --cards is an id
# list where junk that greps down to a number would move a card nobody named. A token
# spelling (`card#12`), a `#`, or anything else non-numeric is a config error here.
if [ -n "$CARDS_IN" ]; then
  CARDS_IN="$(printf '%s' "$CARDS_IN" | tr -d '[:space:]')"
  uint_csv_ok "$CARDS_IN" \
    || die "--cards must be a comma-separated list of numeric card ids, e.g. '5877,5874' (got '$CARDS_IN')"
fi

# SECURITY: .release-pr.json is security-sensitive — the api_base it names is where the
# writeback token (KANBAN_WRITEBACK_TOKEN) is sent. A PR editing api_base to point at an
# attacker host would exfiltrate the token on the next promote CI run. board_id/stage are
# validated numeric below; the RESOLVED api_base — whichever source it came from — is
# host-validated (https + $EXPECT_HOST) before any token is sent (#3570). Treat any api_base
# change in a PR as a credential-scope change.
[ -f "$CONFIG" ] || die "no $CONFIG in this repo"
cfg() { jq -r "$1 // empty" "$CONFIG"; }
BOARD="$(cfg '.promote.board_id')"
STAGE="$(cfg '.promote.released_stage_id')"
REF_RE="$(cfg '.ref_token_regex')"; REF_RE="${REF_RE:-DL-[0-9]+}"

# api_base: $KANBAN_API_BASE FIRST, else the committed .promote.api_base. ONE resolution
# feeding ONE guard — see the ENVIRONMENT block in the header for why the env channel exists
# and why it does not weaken the guard. $API_SRC only ever names the CHANNEL, so reporting it
# discloses nothing the api_base itself does not.
#
# EMPTY == UNSET here, and that is not the card#5144 shape. For a value-taking FLAG, an empty
# value had to become a hard error because it silently selected a DEFAULT PATH that was weaker
# than the flag (an unguarded promote reported as a guarded one). The default path here is the
# committed config value, which goes through the identical host guard — so the worst an empty
# $KANBAN_API_BASE can produce is a REFUSAL on the committed placeholder, never a token sent
# somewhere unvalidated. Treating it as unset is also what the CI wrapper needs: an unset
# repo/org variable renders as the empty string, and "no override" is the correct reading of it.
API="${KANBAN_API_BASE:-}"; API_SRC="\$KANBAN_API_BASE"
if [ -z "$API" ]; then API="$(cfg '.promote.api_base')"; API_SRC="$CONFIG .promote.api_base"; fi

[ -n "$BOARD" ] && [ -n "$STAGE" ] \
  || die ".promote.{board_id,released_stage_id} are required in $CONFIG"
[ -n "$API" ] \
  || die "no api base — set \$KANBAN_API_BASE, or .promote.api_base in $CONFIG"
uint_ok "$BOARD" || die ".promote.board_id must be numeric (got '$BOARD')"
uint_ok "$STAGE" || die ".promote.released_stage_id must be numeric (got '$STAGE')"
TOKEN="${KANBAN_WRITEBACK_TOKEN:-}"
[ -n "$TOKEN" ] || die "KANBAN_WRITEBACK_TOKEN is not set"
API="${API%/}"

# Fail-closed host guard on the RESOLVED api_base BEFORE any token is sent — one call, on
# whichever source won above, because two guarded paths is how one of them ends up unguarded.
# The expected host is $KANBAN_EXPECTED_HOST — REQUIRED, no baked default: this script is
# vendored by operators onto their own kanban hosts, so there is no host to safely assume.
# Pin it in the promote CI env (a repo/org variable — out-of-band from the PR-editable
# .release-pr.json, so an attacker's api_base edit can't also change the constraint). If it
# is unset/empty we refuse BEFORE any token is sent.
# The single trailing dot comes off the DECLARED host here, before the non-empty check, so
# a declaration of "." alone normalises to empty and dies LOUD rather than becoming a
# wildcard — the same order kb_require_known_api_host uses in _kb-board-lib.sh.
# THE ONLY SPELLING OF THE RESOLVED BASE THAT MAY BE RENDERED, resolved once here — after
# the trailing-slash strip, so it is the final value — so every message below inherits the
# redaction rather than having to remember it (card#7500). $API itself is what goes on the
# wire; $API_SHOWN is what goes to stderr.
API_SHOWN="$(redact_userinfo "$API")"

EXPECT_HOST="${KANBAN_EXPECTED_HOST:-}"; EXPECT_HOST="${EXPECT_HOST%.}"
[ -n "$EXPECT_HOST" ] \
  || die "KANBAN_EXPECTED_HOST must be set to the expected api host before sending the writeback token; refusing to send"
host_ok "$API" \
  || die "refusing to send token — api_base '$API_SHOWN' (from $API_SRC) is not https:// on '$EXPECT_HOST' (or a subdomain). A malicious .release-pr.json api_base can exfiltrate KANBAN_WRITEBACK_TOKEN; set KANBAN_EXPECTED_HOST to your kanban host, and \$KANBAN_API_BASE to the api base on it."

# State the CHANNEL the accepted api_base came from, every run. Which source won is the one
# thing a two-source resolution can get wrong that no other output reveals — a stale exported
# $KANBAN_API_BASE silently outranking a corrected config is a run against the wrong board
# with a clean summary. stderr, so the stdout summary line consumers parse stays byte-identical.
#
# THE CHANNEL AND THE EXPECTED HOST, NOT THE RESOLVED BASE — deliberately. host_ok ACCEPTS a
# base carrying userinfo (`https://u:pw@<expected-host>/api/v3` is a legitimate api_base and is
# asserted as accepted in tests/kb-host-guard-selftest.sh), so echoing $API on the SUCCESS path
# would print a credential into every CI run log, on every run. The two facts an operator
# actually needs — which channel won, and what it was checked against — carry neither.
# (The refuse paths print $API_SHOWN — the same base with any userinfo masked by
# redact_userinfo above, adopted at every render site in this tool and in the lib, card#7500.)
echo "promote-released-cards: api_base resolved from $API_SRC, host-guarded against '$EXPECT_HOST'" >&2

# --- resolve the shipped refs (DL tokens + PR numbers) -----------------------
# numlist normalizes the SHIPPED side (a git-log / --dls text stream) to bare integers with
# leading zeros stripped (keeping >=1 digit) + deduped. The CARD side is normalized by the
# `norm` jq def in the correlation below (`sub("^0+(?=.)";"")`) — the SAME leading-zero rule,
# re-expressed in jq because the two sides live in different runtimes (shell stream vs board
# JSON) on opposite sides of one comparison, and this script is a vendored standalone that
# must not source the lib. Keep the two in sync (like the host_ok mirror): "093" and 93 must
# compare equal, so a change to either strip must be mirrored in the other.
numlist() { grep -oE '[0-9]+' | sed 's/^0*\([0-9]\)/\1/' | awk 'NF && !seen[$0]++'; }
CARD_IDS="$(printf '%s' "$CARDS_IN" | numlist || true)"
if [ -n "$DLS_IN" ] || [ -n "$CARDS_IN" ]; then
  DL_NUMS="$(printf '%s' "$DLS_IN" | grep -oiE "$REF_RE" | numlist || true)"
  PR_NUMS=""
else
  HEAD_REF="${HEAD_REF:-HEAD}"
  if [ -z "$BASE" ]; then
    BASE="$(git describe --tags --abbrev=0 "${HEAD_REF}^" 2>/dev/null || true)"
    # A DERIVED baseline uses LOCAL tags. The authoritative CI invocation fetches
    # tags (fetch-depth 0 + fetch-tags), so it is remote-truth by construction; a
    # LOCAL run's tag set can lag a release and silently over-report the shipped
    # range (card #4389 — the release-pr-body #4348 lie, one tool over). Name it
    # rather than fetching here: this is a vendored security tool whose behavior
    # must stay deterministic; the explicit override is --base.
    [ -n "${GITHUB_ACTIONS:-}" ] || echo "promote-released-cards: NOTE — baseline '$BASE' derived from LOCAL tags; if origin has newer release tags this range over-reports. Run 'git fetch --tags' first or pass --base <tag>." >&2
  fi
  # An empty base would make RANGE collapse to just HEAD → git log walks ALL history
  # → every DL/PR ever committed is "shipped" → mass wrong-card move. Refuse it (the
  # first-ever release, or a shallow/tagless clone). Use --base or --dls explicitly.
  [ -n "$BASE" ] || die "no base tag found before $HEAD_REF — refusing a full-history sweep; pass --base <ref> or --dls"
  RANGE="$BASE..$HEAD_REF"
  SUBJECTS="$(git log "$RANGE" --no-merges --format='%s' 2>/dev/null || true)"
  DL_NUMS="$(printf '%s\n' "$SUBJECTS" | grep -oiE "$REF_RE" | numlist || true)"
  # The squash-merge PR marker is the TRAILING (#NNN) — not the inline #card refs.
  PR_NUMS="$(printf '%s\n' "$SUBJECTS" | grep -oE '\(#[0-9]+\)[[:space:]]*$' | numlist || true)"
fi
[ -n "$DL_NUMS$PR_NUMS$CARD_IDS" ] || { echo "promote-released-cards: no shipped refs in range — nothing to do."; exit 0; }
jarr() { printf '%s' "$1" | jq -R . | jq -s 'map(select(. != ""))'; }
DLS_J="$(jarr "$DL_NUMS")"; PRS_J="$(jarr "$PR_NUMS")"; CARDS_J="$(jarr "$CARD_IDS")"

# --- read the WHOLE board (paged) --------------------------------------------
# Auth is fed OUT-OF-BAND via a stdin herestring (-H @- <<<) so the bearer token never
# appears in curl's argv (world-readable via `ps aux` / /proc/<pid>/cmdline on a multi-user
# host, #3569). A herestring also redirects a regular temp file onto fd 0 rather than a
# /dev/fd named pipe, so it works on native mingw64/Git-Bash curl where process substitution
# (-H @<(...)) fails to open its fd (#34); the temp is seekable, so curl's --retry is safe.
# --retry rides through a transient prod outage: the merge-to-main promote job
# races the operator's `bin/deploy.sh`, which drops prod into maintenance mode
# (HTTP 503) for the seconds-long deploy window (#3700). curl's --retry backs off
# exponentially on a transient error only — HTTP 408/429/5xx, a timeout, or (with
# --retry-connrefused) a refused connection — never a 4xx, so a genuine 401/404
# still fails fast. Bounded by --retry-max-time so a truly-down prod still exits.
# Card moves are idempotent (set stage N) and a 503 applies nothing, so retrying
# a PATCH is safe.
api() { curl -fsS --retry 5 --retry-connrefused --retry-max-time 60 -H @- -H "Accept: application/json" "$@" <<<"$(printf 'Authorization: Bearer %s' "$TOKEN")"; }

# fetch_whole_board — read every card on $BOARD via paged search.json and CENSUS the
# result against the server's own meta.total, so a short read can't silently promote
# from an INCOMPLETE scan (the miss this tool exists to make loud). Prints the deduped
# cards JSON array on stdout. This ports the lib fetch_board_cards backstop (#4338)
# WITHOUT sourcing it — the tool stays a self-contained vendored standalone (like the
# host_ok mirror); it is a co-vendored port, kept honest by promote-pagination-selftest.
# Semantics deliberately mirror the lib EXCEPT the failure policy: the lib returns an rc
# for its caller to interpret, whereas this tool IS the caller and its policy — matching
# its own page-cap check just below — is to DIE on an incomplete read rather than promote
# a partial board.
fetch_whole_board() {
  local cards='[]' page=1 resp data n total='' sum_n=0 read_n last_page=''
  while :; do
    resp="$(api "$API/tasks/search.json?q=board_id=$BOARD&limit=200&page=$page")" \
      || die "board $BOARD read failed (HTTP error) — check the token and api_base ($API_SHOWN)"
    if [ "$page" -eq 1 ]; then
      # meta.last_page is a SECONDARY termination signal — the n<200 short-page break is
      # the primary one. It defaults to UNKNOWN (empty), NOT 1: this loop historically
      # paged until a short page, so an ABSENT last_page must fall through to n<200, never
      # break the scan at page 1 on a full 200-row page (that would silently truncate when
      # meta.total is also absent, the very miss #4513 guards). We break on it only when the
      # server actually declares it — the lib's fetch_board_cards now applies the identical
      # guard (card #4623), so this co-vendored copy and the lib agree on last_page semantics.
      last_page="$(printf '%s' "$resp" | jq -r '.meta.last_page // empty' 2>/dev/null)"
      # Usable only as a POSITIVE integer: absent, non-numeric, negative, or <1 all mean
      # "not meaningfully declared" → unknown ('') → fall through to the n<200 break. A
      # last_page of 0 on a full page would otherwise break the scan at page 1 (the same
      # truncation class as an absent value).
      uint_ok "$last_page" || last_page=''
      [ -n "$last_page" ] && [ "$last_page" -lt 1 ] && last_page=''
      total="$(printf '%s' "$resp" | jq -r '.meta.total // empty' 2>/dev/null)"
    fi
    # `.data` must be PRESENT and an ARRAY, on every page. `.data // []` could not tell a
    # page that returned nothing from a page that returned no ANSWER: a 2xx carrying a
    # proxy's JSON error object became `[]`, which is a short page, which ends the loop —
    # so a page-2 fault promoted from a truncated board at rc 0 with nothing on stderr
    # (card#6630; measured here before the fix — page 1 full + page 2 `{"error":…}` gave
    # 200 cards and rc 0). Only page 1 is left to the zero-CARDS die below, which is this
    # tool's own page-1 predicate and DELIBERATELY not the lib's: a mover can treat an
    # empty board as never a working state, a read verb cannot (see fetch_board_cards's
    # parse-site comment in bin/_kb-board-lib.sh). `empty`, not `// []`, for the same
    # reason it is in the lib: the one thing a jq fault can then print is nothing.
    #
    # The `data='[]'` on page 1 is an ACCEPTED collapse, not an oversight: the extraction
    # can now tell an unreadable envelope ('') from a genuinely empty one ('[]'), and this
    # arm deliberately re-merges them so both land on the zero-cards die, whose message
    # names board membership — wrong for a proxy error body, and already wrong for one
    # before this change. Distinguishing them reworks an operator-facing refusal, which is
    # a gated decision; it is tracked on card#6630 in docs/CONSOLIDATION-PLAN.md.
    data="$(printf '%s' "$resp" | jq -c 'if (.data|type) == "array" then .data else empty end')"
    if [ -z "$data" ]; then
      if [ "$page" -eq 1 ]; then
        data='[]'
      else
        die "board $BOARD page $page returned no readable card array (HTTP 2xx) — refusing to promote on an INCOMPLETE board read (a shipped card could be silently missed). Retry."
      fi
    fi
    n="$(printf '%s' "$data" | jq 'length')"
    if [ "$page" -eq 1 ] && [ "${n:-0}" -eq 0 ]; then
      die "board $BOARD returned 0 visible cards — the token's user is likely not a member of board $BOARD (or wrong instance). Refusing."
    fi
    # Concatenate via STDIN, not `--argjson` — a board page can exceed MAX_ARG_STRLEN
    # (~128KB), and passing $data/$cards as a CLI arg dies "Argument list too long"
    # (the #3091 argv-overflow class; board 5 was measured crossing the cap at ~98
    # cards, and every board here has grown well past that since).
    cards="$(printf '%s\n%s\n' "$cards" "$data" | jq -c -s 'add')"
    sum_n=$((sum_n + ${n:-0}))
    [ "${n:-0}" -lt 200 ] && break
    [ -n "$last_page" ] && [ "$page" -ge "$last_page" ] && break
    page=$((page + 1))
    [ "$page" -gt "${PROMOTE_PAGE_CAP:-50}" ] && die "promote-released-cards: result set exceeded PROMOTE_PAGE_CAP=${PROMOTE_PAGE_CAP:-50} (page=$page) — refusing to promote on a truncated board read; raise the cap or narrow"
  done
  # Dedup by id — defends against an API that ignores `page=` (same page each loop) or
  # returns overlapping pages; otherwise a card would be PATCHed once per duplicate.
  # (Reorders; harmless — this is a mover keyed on id, not a display surface.)
  cards="$(printf '%s' "$cards" | jq -c 'unique_by(.id)')"
  read_n="$(printf '%s' "$cards" | jq 'length')"
  # Short-read census (#4338), pre-dedup sum vs meta.total tells a REAL undercount from
  # a page-boundary dedup artifact: sum_n < total ⇒ the server delivered fewer rows than
  # it claims exist ⇒ an incomplete scan that would silently leave a shipped card
  # un-promoted ⇒ REFUSE (die), same policy as the page-cap guard above. sum_n >= total
  # with read_n < total ⇒ one card straddled a page boundary and dedup collapsed it ⇒
  # the read is complete ⇒ warn only, promote proceeds.
  if uint_ok "$total" && [ "$total" -gt "${read_n:-0}" ]; then
    if [ "$sum_n" -lt "$total" ]; then
      die "board $BOARD has $total cards but pages delivered only $sum_n ($read_n after dedup) — refusing to promote on an INCOMPLETE board read (a shipped card could be silently missed). Retry; if the board is genuinely larger than the page cap, raise PROMOTE_PAGE_CAP."
    fi
    echo "promote-released-cards: NOTE — read $read_n distinct of $total ($sum_n across pages); a card straddled a page boundary and dedup collapsed it — read complete." >&2
  fi
  printf '%s' "$cards"
}
CARDS="$(fetch_whole_board)"

# --- correlate: a card ships if its dl_number, pr_number OR own id is in the shipped sets
PLAN="$(printf '%s' "$CARDS" | jq -c --argjson dls "$DLS_J" --argjson prs "$PRS_J" --argjson cids "$CARDS_J" '
  # normalize a card ref (dl_number, pr_number or id) to the bare-integer, zero-stripped
  # form numlist produces for the shipped side, so "093"/93 correlate. Single-sourced so the
  # ref fields cannot strip differently.
  def norm: (. // "")|tostring|gsub("[^0-9]";"")|sub("^0+(?=.)";"");
  [ .[]
    | . as $c
    | ($c.payload.dl_number | norm) as $cdl
    | ($c.payload.pr_number | norm) as $cpr
    | ($c.id | norm) as $cid
    | (($cdl != "") and ($dls|index($cdl) != null)) as $byDl
    | (($cpr != "") and ($prs|index($cpr) != null)) as $byPr
    | (($cid != "") and ($cids|index($cid) != null)) as $byId
    | select($byDl or $byPr or $byId)
    # Display precedence keeps DL first so a run with no --cards renders exactly as before.
    | { id: $c.id, stage: ($c.workflow_stage_id|tostring),
        ref: (if $byDl then "DL-\($cdl)" elif $byId then "card#\($cid)" else "#\($cpr)" end),
        dl: (if $byDl then $cdl else null end),
        pr: (if $byPr then $cpr else null end),
        card: (if $byId then $cid else null end) } ]')"

# A shipped DL or CARD ID with no matching card is the high-value miss (a decision whose
# card is unstamped/absent, or an id naming no card on this board) — warn on it. Unmatched
# PRs are NOT reported: a PR is only a correlation fallback and routinely has no card (dep
# bumps, release commits), so warning on them is cry-wolf noise.
#
# Both kinds go into ONE list so the single WARNING line stays the whole no-card report —
# release-pr-body's coverage section reads that one line, and a second line would give it a
# report it silently half-read. With --cards absent the card leg is empty and the list is
# byte-identical to the DL-only one.
MATCHED_DLS="$(printf '%s' "$PLAN" | jq -c '[.[].dl|select(.!=null)]|unique')"
MATCHED_CARDS="$(printf '%s' "$PLAN" | jq -c '[.[].card|select(.!=null)]|unique')"
MISSING="$(jq -c -n --argjson sd "$DLS_J" --argjson md "$MATCHED_DLS" \
                    --argjson sc "$CARDS_J" --argjson mc "$MATCHED_CARDS" '
  [ $sd[] | select(. as $x | ($md|index($x))|not) | "DL-\(.)" ]
  + [ $sc[] | select(. as $x | ($mc|index($x))|not) | "card#\(.)" ]')"

# --- move --------------------------------------------------------------------
moved=0; skipped=0; failed=0; guarded=0
while IFS= read -r row; do
  [ -n "$row" ] || continue
  id="$(printf '%s' "$row" | jq -r '.id')"
  cur="$(printf '%s' "$row" | jq -r '.stage')"
  ref="$(printf '%s' "$row" | jq -r '.ref')"
  if [ "$cur" = "$STAGE" ]; then echo "  = $ref (#$id): already released"; skipped=$((skipped + 1)); continue; fi
  # Source-stage guard (opt-in via --shipped-stages). Promotion means Shipped→Released, so a
  # matched card NOT in a Shipped-class source stage is skipped, not moved — promotion never
  # resurrects a declined/backlog card carrying a stale or recycled DL/PR stamp. Evaluated
  # AFTER the already-released check so an idempotent re-run of a genuinely-released card still
  # reads as "already released" (its now-Released stage is not a Shipped-class one).
  if [ -n "$SHIPPED_STAGES" ] && ! in_shipped_stage "$cur"; then
    echo "  ⊘ $ref (#$id): current stage $cur not in a Shipped-class source stage — skipped; promotion never resurrects declined/backlog cards" >&2
    guarded=$((guarded + 1)); continue
  fi
  if [ "$DRY" = 1 ]; then echo "  → $ref (#$id): would move $cur → $STAGE"; moved=$((moved + 1)); continue; fi
  if api -X PATCH -H "Content-Type: application/json" -d "$(jq -nc --argjson s "$STAGE" '{workflow_stage_id: $s}')" "$API/tasks/$id.json" >/dev/null; then
    echo "  ✓ $ref (#$id): moved $cur → $STAGE"; moved=$((moved + 1))
  else
    echo "  ✗ $ref (#$id): move failed (left in place)" >&2; failed=$((failed + 1))
  fi
done <<< "$(printf '%s' "$PLAN" | jq -c '.[]')"

nmiss="$(printf '%s' "$MISSING" | jq 'length')"
if [ "${nmiss:-0}" -gt 0 ]; then
  echo "promote-released-cards: WARNING — $nmiss shipped ref(s) matched NO card (unstamped card, or none): $(printf '%s' "$MISSING" | jq -r 'join(", ")')" >&2
fi
# The stage-guarded count is surfaced only when the guard is active, so a run with no
# --shipped-stages keeps a byte-identical summary line (backward compatibility).
echo "promote-released-cards: $moved moved, $skipped already-released, ${SHIPPED_STAGES:+$guarded stage-guarded, }$nmiss no-card, $failed failed$([ "$DRY" = 1 ] && printf ' (dry-run)')."
# The guard's state, ALWAYS, on its own stderr line. #5144 made an explicitly-EMPTY
# --shipped-stages die, but the ABSENT case still printed a summary indistinguishable from
# a guarded run — so an unguarded promote read as clean, which is the same "looks enabled,
# isn't" shape the guard exists to prevent, one layer out.
#
# Deliberately NOT folded into the summary line above: that line is byte-identical when the
# guard is absent by an explicit v0.21.0 fleet-adoption decision, anything parsing it would
# break, and a selftest asserts it. Reporting on a SEPARATE stderr line gets the visibility
# without overturning that decision — the summary stays a stable parse target, and stderr is
# where this tool already puts its operator-facing findings (the no-card WARNING above, the
# per-card guard skips).
if [ -n "$SHIPPED_STAGES" ]; then
  echo "promote-released-cards: stage-guard ON (Shipped-class source stages: $SHIPPED_STAGES) — a matched card outside them is skipped, never promoted." >&2
else
  echo "promote-released-cards: stage-guard OFF (no --shipped-stages) — every DL/PR-matched card was promoted regardless of its current stage, so a stale or recycled stamp on a declined/backlog card would have moved it. Pass --shipped-stages <ids> to guard." >&2
fi
# --- exit policy: a "0 promoted" run is told apart by its ROOT CAUSE ----------
# Systemic move failure (a card matched but every PATCH errored) → exit 1.
if [ "$failed" -gt 0 ] && [ "$moved" = 0 ] && [ "$skipped" = 0 ]; then exit 1; fi
# Newly promoted NOTHING (moved==0) despite a non-empty shipped-ref set (an empty set
# already exited "nothing to do"). Two distinct causes, told apart by whether ref-
# derivation was COMPLETE:
#   - complete (an explicit --dls/--cards set, or a MERGE-commit release tip whose range carries every
#     feature/prep subject) → the derived DLs are genuinely cardless (docs-only, or tracked
#     elsewhere) → the WARNING above suffices, exit 0. An idempotent re-run of a legit
#     merge-commit release (everything already at target, skipped>0) lands here too.
#   - INCOMPLETE (a non-merge tip → the release PR was squash/rebase-merged, collapsing the
#     per-PR subjects this reads) → cards exist but went unmatched (the v0.32.0 silent-green
#     incident) → FAIL loud so the operator verifies + promotes by hand.
# NOTE: `skipped` is deliberately NOT in the guard — a squash release whose siblings were
# already promoted (a partial/earlier run) still has moved==0 and must FAIL; the legit
# merge-commit re-run is exempted by the tip check, not by skipped. `guarded` is NOT in
# the guard either: a source-stage-skipped card proves only that a DL token survived the
# squash — the die guards PR-marker completeness, and the squashed feature commits' (#NNN)
# markers can be lost while a title DL still matches. A guarded-skip therefore cannot
# disprove a dropped sibling ref; on this off-process path (non-merge tip only) the
# ~2-minute manual verification a false alarm costs is the right side of the asymmetry
# vs a silently un-promoted shipped card.
if [ "$moved" = 0 ] && [ -z "$DLS_IN$CARDS_IN" ] && ! merge_commit_tip "$HEAD_REF"; then
  die "FAILED — 0 cards newly promoted and the release tip (${HEAD_REF}) is not a merge commit. A squash/rebase merge of the release PR may have dropped the per-PR refs this derives from (use a MERGE commit for release/sync PRs — VERSIONING.md). Verify and promote manually."
fi
exit 0
